WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:03.009
Jeffrey Reddick, screenwriter: For me it’s
just like trying…I have a movie at Lionsgate

00:00:03.009 --> 00:00:09.450
that’s got a 95% African American cast and
it’s not about…it’s a horror movie…it’s

00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:10.570
a slasher movie.

00:00:10.570 --> 00:00:14.460
It’s just like if you’re watching SCREAM
where it’s a bunch of pretty White kids

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:16.990
and there’s not even the first one (they
didn’t have any Black people in the first

00:00:16.990 --> 00:00:21.940
one) but one of the later SCREAMs where you
are watching a bunch of pretty White kids

00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:24.670
and they have the one Black friend, our movie
is like you’re just focusing on all the

00:00:24.670 --> 00:00:27.200
pretty Black and Latino kids with a couple
white friends.

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:31.290
So it’s not that you’re making a movie
that is about race, it’s just you’re casting

00:00:31.290 --> 00:00:36.620
a movie that is focusing on a different group
of friends that most of the horror films do.

00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:41.470
So I think that that type of movie (in my
humble opinion) will actually reach a wider

00:00:41.470 --> 00:00:46.210
market because white audiences just want to
go see a good horror movie, you know?

00:00:46.210 --> 00:00:50.350
Like they don’t care what color the characters
are.

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:54.641
And the people of color who are horribly under
represented in horror films are a huge audience

00:00:54.641 --> 00:00:55.641
for horror films.

00:00:55.641 --> 00:00:58.399
We’ve just done all the test…even at New
Line [Cinema] we just did test marketing back

00:00:58.399 --> 00:01:04.670
in the 90’s and it was in all the major
cities it was 50% of the audiences in all

00:01:04.670 --> 00:01:06.500
of the major cities were African American
and Latino.

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:11.360
And it’s such a horribly underserved market
that they will go to see the movie numerous

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:17.000
times because they’ve never seen themselves
represented on screen as just people.

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:19.970
And regular horror fans (no, not regular…that
so awful).

00:01:19.970 --> 00:01:22.140
I’m trying to be so PC right now.

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:23.370
Screw that!

00:01:23.370 --> 00:01:25.220
I’m no snowflake.

00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:28.530
But you know horror fans (white horror fans)
as long as it’s a good slasher movie, they

00:01:28.530 --> 00:01:31.650
are going to go see it a couple times, too.

00:01:31.650 --> 00:01:34.051
So that’s going to be a really good social
experiment.

00:01:34.051 --> 00:01:39.640
And it’s taken me about 20…I tried to
have a Black lead in THE FINAL DESTINATION

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:41.939
movies and it just never happened.

00:01:41.939 --> 00:01:49.710
And so it’s taken me 20-some years to finally
get a movie going.

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:57.829
So it’s funny when we talk about where race
is in America and it’s like “Oh, we voted

00:01:57.829 --> 00:02:00.720
for Obama so we’re post-racial now and everything
is honkey-dory.”

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:01.720
And it’s like “Ah?”

00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:05.390
Because Hollywood is pretty freakin’ liberal.

00:02:05.390 --> 00:02:09.920
But they know what sells and what wont sell
and how people pigeon-hole that kind of movies

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:21.130
and sometimes they do or what they will go
see but sometimes you just have to prove to

00:02:21.130 --> 00:02:26.330
them like a movie like GIRL TRIP that came
out with Jada Pinkett and Queen Latifah and

00:02:26.330 --> 00:02:29.480
makes 30 million dollars opening weekend and
it’s like “What?”

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:33.660
And WONDER WOMAN comes out directed by a woman
about a woman superhero and you are like “What?”

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:37.650
They act so surprised and its like nobody
wants to take a chance on something that is

00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:38.650
different.

00:02:38.650 --> 00:02:46.100
So you have to find that person who will and
then once you do, I guarantee once this movie

00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:50.750
comes out there’s going to be a whole bunch
of slasher/horror films that are like with

00:02:50.750 --> 00:02:54.450
a heavily diverse cast.

00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:56.110
So again it’s kind of like a quiet fight.

00:02:56.110 --> 00:03:01.270
I haven’t been running around after every
film that’s come out “I wanted a Black

00:03:01.270 --> 00:03:04.060
character to be the lead and they wouldn’t
do it."

00:03:04.060 --> 00:03:05.170
That’s not going to do me any good.

00:03:05.170 --> 00:03:09.340
That’s just going to upset people and make
people think that I’m crying racism, you

00:03:09.340 --> 00:03:10.340
know what I’m saying.

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:11.340
It’s not going to be productive.

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:15.290
So you just kind of have to put your best
foot forward and put your money where your

00:03:15.290 --> 00:03:20.200
mouth is and try to until you find the people
who get behind the movie like the one we’re

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:21.200
doing now.

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:25.930
Film Courage: So did you try and add a non-white
character to any of THE FINAL DESTINATION

00:03:25.930 --> 00:03:26.930
movies?

00:03:26.930 --> 00:03:27.930
Jeffrey: Yeah.

00:03:27.930 --> 00:03:28.930
Film Courage: And what would happen?

00:03:28.930 --> 00:03:29.930
Jeffrey: They would just cast a white person.

00:03:29.930 --> 00:03:32.780
Film Courage: So you would write it into the
script as to who this person was?

00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:33.780
Jeffrey: Yeah.

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:39.239
Film Courage: And then when you saw the final
product that person wasn’t there?

00:03:39.239 --> 00:03:40.340
Jeffrey: Yeah.

00:03:40.340 --> 00:03:46.590
And the reality is internationally movies
if the lead is a person of color they don’t

00:03:46.590 --> 00:03:50.110
sell as well internationally.

00:03:50.110 --> 00:03:56.960
And again except for Halle Berry or Denzel
Washington, there’s like maybe five names

00:03:56.960 --> 00:04:00.420
that will in certain genres that will sell
internationally.

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:04.849
But a movie like GET OUT comes out and it
makes a hundred million here and it only cost

00:04:04.849 --> 00:04:05.849
five million to make.

00:04:05.849 --> 00:04:10.420
It’s like who cares what it makes internationally.

00:04:10.420 --> 00:04:16.569
So there is a little bit of business reasoning
behind it but a lot of it is like until you

00:04:16.569 --> 00:04:21.119
try to break the mold, you never know what
the mold…you never know until you try to

00:04:21.119 --> 00:04:22.189
break the mold.

00:04:22.189 --> 00:04:26.369
That’s why I think this last year with GET
OUT and WONDER WOMAN were so important is

00:04:26.369 --> 00:04:31.340
because even on my Facebook before it came
I was like “I bet my life WONDER WOMAN is

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:37.639
going to be one of the top selling superhero
films of all time” and it’s just people

00:04:37.639 --> 00:04:43.050
underestimate how powerful of an impact Lynda
Carter had as Wonder Woman, so pretty much

00:04:43.050 --> 00:04:50.419
everybody in their forties, fifties and above
and late thirties…anybody who kind of grew

00:04:50.419 --> 00:04:54.360
up on seeing Lynda Carter as Wonder Woman
and loved that character and she’s been

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:57.640
around forever and then you’ve got younger
people meeting her in the Justice League.

00:04:57.640 --> 00:05:03.599
So I knew the movie was going to do huge business
but nobody…it’s like if an openly gay

00:05:03.599 --> 00:05:07.020
actor were to play James Bond, nobody would
believe it.

00:05:07.020 --> 00:05:09.520
I’m like “Why don’t we try it first?”

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:14.740
Because I think people who go watch movies
know that Robert Englund is not a real-life

00:05:14.740 --> 00:05:20.639
bad guy that wears…I’m sure they know
that he’s not like that in real life.

00:05:20.639 --> 00:05:25.361
It’s funny so you just try to make whatever
changes you can with your art and a lot of

00:05:25.361 --> 00:05:26.860
times it doesn’t even happen.

00:05:26.860 --> 00:05:35.810
And again, I work in the horror genre so it’s
even harder to try and like…you can’t

00:05:35.810 --> 00:05:38.259
get too high-minded in a horror film.

00:05:38.259 --> 00:05:46.470
I can make strong female characters, I can
make interesting dynamics between everybody,

00:05:46.470 --> 00:05:52.741
show diversities of people of every religion
or background and you can try to put all of

00:05:52.741 --> 00:05:57.689
that in your movie but a lot of times that
ends up getting out of your hands once you

00:05:57.689 --> 00:06:03.819
sell the script, so that’s where you just
have to kind of let go and be grateful you

00:06:03.819 --> 00:06:05.310
had a movie made.

